---
import Page from "../../layouts/Page.astro";
import Terminal from "../../components/Terminal.astro";
import GetSalvage from "../../components/GetSalvage.astro";
import install from "../../examples/lldb-install.txt?raw";
import uninstall from "../../examples/lldb-uninstall.txt?raw";
import before from "../../examples/lldb-before.txt?raw";
import after from "../../examples/lldb-after.txt?raw";

// The types salvage_unreal.py covers (crates/salvage/lldb/README.md).
const covered = [
  ["Strings and containers", ["FString", "FUtf8String", "FName", "FText", "TStringView", "TArray", "TArrayView"]],
  ["Pointers", ["TSharedPtr", "TSharedRef", "TWeakPtr", "TUniquePtr", "TObjectPtr", "FSoftObjectPath", "TSoftObjectPtr", "TSoftClassPtr"]],
  ["Other types", ["TOptional", "TVariant", "FGuid", "FGameplayTag"]],
  ["Math", ["FVector", "FVector2D", "FVector4", "FRotator", "FQuat", "FTransform", "FIntPoint", "FIntVector", "FColor", "FLinearColor"]],
] as const;
const stand = "Salvage's own test fixture: small stand-in types with the same names and members as Unreal's, built with clang and stopped in LLDB on a Mac.";
---
<Page
  title="Read Unreal types in the debugger"
  compact
  description="How salvage lldb makes Xcode and LLDB show Unreal Engine types as values, what it changes on your machine, and how to take it out."
  dek="Out of the box, Xcode and LLDB show an FString as a pile of members and a TObjectPtr as a pointer to a pointer. salvage lldb install teaches them to show the values instead."
>
  <div class="prose wide">
    <h2>Before and after</h2>
    <Terminal
      prompt="(lldb)"
      command="frame variable greeting ship_ptr text tag"
      output={before}
      marks={false}
      caption={`Without formatters. ${stand}`}
    />
    <Terminal
      prompt="(lldb)"
      command="frame variable greeting ship_ptr text tag position shared_crate"
      output={after}
      marks={false}
      caption={`With Salvage's formatters loaded. ${stand}`}
    />

    <h2>Install it</h2>
    <Terminal command="salvage lldb install" output={install} caption="Real output, run in an empty test home folder." />
    <p>
      Restart Xcode, or any LLDB session you have open, and the formatters load with it.
      Running <code>salvage lldb install</code> again changes nothing. If the formatter script has changed since, it updates
      the script.
    </p>

    <h2>What it changes</h2>
    <p>Unlike <code>doctor</code> and <code>soundings</code>, this one does change your machine, in two places:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        It writes the formatter script, <code>salvage_unreal.py</code>, to Salvage's data
        folder: <code>~/Library/Application Support/Salvage/lldb/</code> on a Mac.
      </li>
      <li>
        It adds one line that loads the script to <code>~/.lldbinit</code>, which LLDB reads, and
        to <code>~/.lldbinit-Xcode</code> if you have one, since Xcode reads that instead. A comment above the line says
        where it came from.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Before it changes either file, it backs it up next to the original, as <code>.lldbinit.salvage-backup-</code> followed by a number. It never rewrites your own lines, and if your init file is a symlink, it edits the file the
      link points to.
    </p>

    <h2>Take it out</h2>
    <Terminal command="salvage lldb uninstall" output={uninstall} caption="Real output, from the same test home folder." />
    <p>Uninstall removes the script and Salvage's lines, and backs the file up again first. Your own lines stay.</p>

    <h2>What it covers</h2>
    <dl class="covered">
      {covered.map(([group, types]) => (
        <div>
          <dt>{group}</dt>
          <dd>{types.map((t, i) => <><code>{t}</code>{i < types.length - 1 && " "}</>)}</dd>
        </div>
      ))}
    </dl>

    <h2 id="epics-formatters-and-ours">Epic's formatters, and ours</h2>
    <p>Unreal Engine ships LLDB formatters of its own, under Epic's license, in the engine's <code>Engine/Extras/LLDBDataFormatters</code> folder (<code>UEDataFormatters.py</code> and a variant for 2-byte characters). In 5.8.4 they cover <code>FString</code>, <code>FName</code>, <code>TArray</code>, <code>TMap</code>, <code>TSet</code>, <code>TWeakObjectPtr</code>, and Unreal's sparse, bit and chunked arrays.</p>
    <p>Salvage's formatters overlap with Epic's on <code>FString</code>, <code>FName</code> and <code>TArray</code>. What they add is everything else in the list above: the shared, unique, weak and object pointers, soft references, <code>FText</code>, <code>TOptional</code>, <code>TVariant</code>, <code>FGuid</code>, <code>FGameplayTag</code> and the math types, as open source under the MIT license.</p>
    <p>You can load both. If Epic's are already in your init file, keep them: they cover <code>TMap</code>, <code>TSet</code> and the rest, which Salvage's don't. <code>salvage lldb install</code> adds its line after any you already have, so where both cover a type, Salvage's loads last and wins. <a href="/charts/salvage-doctor/"><code>salvage doctor</code></a> checks your init file and tells you which formatters it loads.</p>

    <h2>How it was written</h2>
    <p>
      The formatters were written from scratch for Salvage, from documented behavior and the names of each type's
      members. They contain no code from the engine's formatters or headers. Each one looks members up by name and
      falls back to LLDB's usual display if anything doesn't match, so an engine version with a different layout shows
      raw values, not errors.
    </p>
    <p>
      CI checks every formatter on macOS, against stand-in types with both the game and editor layouts
      of <code>FName</code>.
    </p>

    <GetSalvage />
  </div>
</Page>
<style>
  .covered {
    margin: var(--s-4) 0 0;
  }
  .covered > div {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 11rem minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: var(--s-4);
    padding-block: var(--s-3);
    border-top: 2px solid var(--rule);
  }
  .covered dt {
    font-stretch: var(--stretch-sign);
    font-weight: 750;
  }
  .covered dd {
    margin: 0;
    line-height: 2;
  }
  @media (max-width: 600px) {
    .covered > div {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
      gap: var(--s-1);
    }
  }
</style>
